WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.600
Automatic differentiation follows operation dependencies and applies local chain-rule rules. It computes derivatives of the implemented

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:15.000
function, which may differ from the function you intended because of a wrong reduction, axis, detached

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.850
value or graph construction.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:24.548
Use float64 for this small numerical check. Smoothness near the test point avoids ambiguity from activation

00:00:24.548 --> 00:00:24.997
kinks.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:32.916
A numerical comparison is useful only when the objective, reduction and tested parameter match the analytic

00:00:32.916 --> 00:00:33.380
calculation.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:40.247
Will making the step arbitrarily tiny always make a numerical derivative more accurate? Pause here and

00:00:40.247 --> 00:00:42.230
make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:51.104
Evaluate both displaced inputs while keeping all other inputs fixed. A central difference has smaller truncation

00:00:51.104 --> 00:00:58.874
error than a one-sided difference for this smooth example. For our starting inputs, Step size h:

00:00:58.874 --> 00:01:02.273
0.01. Analytic gradient: 12. Numerical gradient: 12.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.342
For this cubic the truncation error is exactly h squared. At extremely tiny h, subtraction and

00:01:09.342 --> 00:01:13.430
floating-point rounding can dominate, so smaller is not always better.

00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:20.997
Numerical agreement tests a local computation. It does not establish that the overall model, data protocol

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:22.763
or objective is appropriate.

